# November 19, 2026: western Delaware County, New York (the West Branch of the Delaware and the Susquehanna side):
# Walton, Hamden, Tompkins (Trout Creek, Cannonsville), Franklin, Sidney, Deposit, Hancock (East Branch,
# Lordville, Rock Valley), with the Hankins stone arch over the line in Sullivan County. Drawn at random by
# tools/choose.py --state ny (region cat), which offered Narrowsburg, Walton and Tannersville; the Atlas had four places
# within 25 km of Walton. Mostly standing buildings, with the drowned Cannonsville hamlets and a stone arch bridge.
# Sources opened in this session: Wikipedia as raw text (tools/wiki_raw.py, including the Delaware County Register list),
# National Register forms through NPGallery (Second Walton Armory, First Congregational Church of Walton, Common School
# 32, Hamden District No. 1 School, First Presbyterian Church of Deposit), and through tools/page.py the HMdb markers,
# the NY State Military Museum's armory page, castleonthedelaware.com, Cinema Treasures, Clio, Upstater, the Town of
# Franklin's Stone Hall page, Franklin Stage Company, the I LOVE NY listing for the Hamden schoolhouse and the Catskill
# Watershed Corporation's Cannonsville boating sheet.
from data_b import N
from data_ma import W
def L(t,u): return [t,u]
def NP(r): return ['National Register form (NPGallery)','https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NRHP/'+r+'_text']
def HM(m,t): return ['HMdb: '+t,'https://www.hmdb.org/m.asp?m='+m]

BATCH={'key':'cat_fpix','label':'Western Delaware County, NY: Walton, Franklin, Sidney, Deposit and the Cannonsville hamlets','added':'2026-11-19 06:39'}

PLACES=[
N('walton-armories','Walton\'s two armories: the Grange hall and the Castle on the Delaware','Walton','cat','civic','standing',42.16549,-75.13047,'exact','view','walk','','1886–1896',
  'Two National Guard armories stand side by side on Stockton Avenue. The first, built in 1886 to a design by Randall and Gilbert of Walton, held the 33rd Separate Company until 1896 and then served as a school and as Walton Grange No. 1454. Next door is Isaac Perry\'s castle-style armory, nearly a twin of the one in Amsterdam: its Register form dates it to 1895, the state military museum to 1895–1896 and an Upstater article to 1897. The Guard left it in 1965; it became the White Castle department store in 1966, later a restaurant and a health club, and was bought in 2006 by Mustafa Sav, who runs it as the Castle on the Delaware.',
  'The 1896 armory is a massive brick building with rough-hewn limestone trim, crenellated towers, a sally port and filled-in rifle slits; the Register form says the drill hall has been opened up again with its end balconies, and that much original finish survives inside (coffered ceilings, wall panelling, original doors and two of three fireplaces). The 1886 armory (now the Grange) is the smaller building to the east. The interiors are seen only at the Castle\'s events and meals; Grange interior not checked.',
  'Two generations of upstate armory, one beside the other.',
  'Both are private. The Castle on the Delaware (139 Stockton Avenue, State Route 206) is a privately owned event venue that holds ticketed dinners and other events (its site lists them); there is no open visiting. The Grange hall at 137 is not open to visit as far as checked. View from Stockton Avenue; per the Register form the Castle\'s lots are west and south of the building. Hours not checked.',
  [W('Walton Grange No. 1454'),NP('16000591'),L('NY State Military Museum: Stockton Avenue Armory','https://museum.dmna.ny.gov/armories-arsenals/walton-armory-stockton-avenue-1896-1965'),L('Castle on the Delaware: about','https://castleonthedelaware.com/about/'),HM('260704','Castle on the Delaware')],conf='mixed',brick='yes'),
N('first-congregational-walton','First Congregational Church of Walton','Walton','cat','worship','standing',42.17076,-75.12760,'exact','view','walk','','1840',
  'The congregation was Walton\'s first religious body. Its church, a wood-frame building with clapboards and a three-stage tower, was built in 1840 and lengthened by sixteen feet in 1862; in 1881 the small-paned windows gave way to leaded coloured glass, and in 1931 the Doric portico and the church house were added. Listed on the National Register in 2015.',
  'Per the Register form: the 1881 windows, each with a stained-glass panel holding a religious symbol in a circle; ash pews in two rows below and curved pews in the gallery; a raised platform with organ pipes behind it. The church house has a wide exterior brick chimney. Interior seen by the public at services only as far as checked; times not checked.',
  '',
  'A working church at 4 Mead Street; look from the street. Service times not checked. Street parking in the village.',
  [W('First Congregational Church of Walton'),NP('15000476')],brick='?'),
N('christ-church-walton','Christ Episcopal Church, Walton','Walton','cat','worship','standing',42.16990,-75.12863,'exact','view','walk','','1832',
  'A Federal Gothic church built in 1832 (Wikipedia\'s category gives 1834) and given a dress of Victorian decoration in 1883. It keeps the meeting-house plan: vestibule, nave, chancel and sacristy under a steep gable, with a central tower and wooden belfry. Listed on the National Register in 1999.',
  'The wood-frame church with its entrance tower and belfry on Gardiner Place. Interior not checked. The Register marker that stood on Gardiner Place is reported missing (HMdb).',
  '',
  'A church on Gardiner Place in the village centre; look from the street. Whether it is still in use and service times not checked. Street parking.',
  [W('Christ Episcopal Church (Walton, New York)'),HM('149422','Christ Church')],conf='mixed',brick='no'),
N('walton-theatre-gardiner-place','Walton Theatre and Gardiner Place','Walton','cat','civic','standing',42.16887,-75.12829,'exact','open','walk','','1914',
  'Walton\'s village hall, with its opera hall, burned on December 10, 1912. Voters, women among them, approved a new hall and theatre, which opened on April 21, 1914 to a standing-room crowd (Cinema Treasures gives the old hall as built about 1883). Films were shown from 1914 and became regular after the house was leased to William Smalley in 1923. The theatre, the Ogden Free Library and the 1936–37 post office make up the Gardiner Place Historic District, listed in 1984.',
  'The theatre front has a pedimented portico on four Corinthian columns. Restoration has been going on for years under a not-for-profit preservation association; Upstater wrote that money was still being raised for seats, ceiling and floors. The Colonial Revival post office next door stands on a limestone-clad base. The theatre is open to ticket holders at its films and shows; library and post office in their own hours (not checked).',
  'A 1914 village-hall theatre still running films and shows.',
  'Owner not checked. The theatre is at 30 Gardiner Place and holds public films, plays and concerts (tickets through its own site). Village parking lots on North Street and Gardiner Place.',
  [L('Cinema Treasures: Walton Theatre','https://www.cinematreasures.org/theaters/21621'),L('Clio: Walton Theater','https://theclio.com/entry/14434'),W('Gardiner Place Historic District'),W('United States Post Office (Walton, New York)'),L('Upstater: Walton Armory and Walton Theatre','https://upstater.com/?p=14875')],conf='mixed',brick='?'),
N('trout-creek-school-32','Common School 32, Trout Creek','Trout Creek','cat','civic','standing',42.20592,-75.27901,'site','view','walk','','1860s',
  'Trout Creek\'s one-room school. District 32 was formed in 1852; the Register form thinks this building went up between 1860 and 1880. It taught until 1968, when it was the last one-room school in the Town of Tompkins to close. The town took it over in 1973 and it has been a senior citizens\' centre since. Listed on the National Register in 2017.',
  'A three-bay clapboarded school with a steep gable on a fieldstone foundation, nine-over-six windows and a six-panel door; the original woodshed survives on the lot. The form describes the single schoolroom as intact, with built-in shelves and blackboards and 1920s hanging lights. Interior open only as a senior centre; hours not checked. A Pomeroy Foundation marker stands by it.',
  '',
  'Owned by the Town of Tompkins and used as a senior centre, at 25 Bridge Street in the hamlet. Look from the street. Visiting hours not checked.',
  [NP('100000811'),HM('289450','Common School District 32')],brick='no'),
N('hamden-district-1-school','Hamden District No. 1 Schoolhouse Museum','Hawleys','cat','civic','standing',42.16821,-75.02302,'exact','open','drive','','1859',
  'A Greek Revival one-room school built in 1859 on a quarter-acre bought for ten dollars; the district\'s first school (1828) stood across the road. It taught until 1954. The Town of Hamden bought it from the Sprague family in 2014 and restored it by 2017, and it was listed on the National Register in 2019 as the town\'s schoolhouse museum.',
  'A timber-framed, clapboarded school on a dry-laid fieldstone foundation, with Doric pilasters at the corners, a full pediment, six-over-six windows and a wood shake roof restored to match the original; a boulder carries the two original stone steps. The woodshed survives. Inside: the schoolroom, open as a museum.',
  '',
  'Owned by the Town of Hamden since 2014 (Register form): a town museum at 5594 East River Road (County Route 1). The I LOVE NY listing says it opens on weekends and one weekday from May to October, and by appointment at any time; the town\'s site lists the hours (not checked).',
  [NP('100003152'),L('I LOVE NY: Hamden District 1 Schoolhouse Museum','https://www.iloveny.com/listing/hamden-district-1-schoolhouse-museum/137741/')],brick='?'),
N('franklin-stone-hall','Stone Hall of the Delaware Literary Institute, Franklin','Franklin','cat','inst','standing',42.33917,-75.16783,'exact','view','walk','','1856',
  'The Delaware Literary Institute was chartered in 1835 and opened its first building, the four-storey "old" Stone Hall, in 1836. That hall burned on March 9, 1856. Its stone replacement, three storeys and 42 by 93 feet with a cupola, is dated 1855–1856 by Wikipedia and 1857 by the town and the marker. The school declined after the Civil War and became a union free school; the building was given up in 1932. In the late 1970s the school board proposed pulling it down for want of a roof, and volunteers raised the money for a metal roof, which began Old Franklin Day. Chapel Hall, built in the same years, is the institute\'s only other survivor.',
  'The three-storey stone hall with its octagonal cupola, porches at the two front doors and a marker between them. The town says it is now used for storage and its interior is dated: interior not open. Chapel Hall nearby is home to the Franklin Stage Company, which gives admission-free summer theatre there.',
  'A grand stone schoolhouse waiting for a use.',
  'View from Center Street and Institute Street in the village of Franklin. Owner not checked; the building is not open. Chapel Hall is open at Franklin Stage Company performances (season dates on its site, not checked). Street parking.',
  [W('New Stone Hall'),W('Delaware Literary Institute'),L('Town of Franklin: "New" Stone Hall','https://franklindelco-ny.gov/download_file/view/c9241e34-eed6-4506-8014-b065b58368e8/265'),HM('254724','Stone Hall'),L('Franklin Stage Company','https://franklinstagecompany.org/')],conf='mixed',brick='no'),
N('sidney-pioneer-cemetery','Pioneer Cemetery and the 1808 church, Sidney','Sidney','cat','burial','standing',42.31640,-75.39352,'exact','view','walk','cemeteries','1787',
  'Sidney\'s community burial ground, with burials from about 1787 to 1890 and about 275 recorded; a Pomeroy marker says at least eight Revolutionary War veterans were buried here between 1792 and 1839. Beside it, the First Congregational Church of Sidney (first called the First Church of Christ) opened in 1808 and closed at the end of 2019. Both are in the Sidney Historic District (listed 2013), which also takes in the 1798 Johnston-Clum house and an archaeological site of 850 BC to 1400 AD.',
  'Early gravestones in the small cemetery at Main and Bridge Streets, with the Register sign and the Patriot Burials marker; the church\'s marker is at the same corner. Church interior not checked; it is closed as a church.',
  '',
  'The cemetery is on Main Street (County Route 23) at Bridge Street in the village, with the Sidney Historical Association\'s sign; see it from the street. Who owns and maintains it, and whether visitors may walk in, not checked. Parking on the village streets.',
  [W('Pioneer Cemetery (Sidney, New York)'),W('Sidney Historic District'),HM('92596','Sidney Plains'),HM('255712','Patriot Burials'),HM('297623','First Church In Sidney')],brick='?'),
N('deposit-first-presbyterian','First Presbyterian Church of Deposit','Deposit','cat','worship','standing',42.06317,-75.42245,'exact','view','walk','','1880',
  'A brick church on a cut-stone foundation, designed by Lawrence B. Valk and dedicated on May 11, 1880, with a session room added in 1889 to match. Valk built auditorium-plan churches for small congregations; this one\'s tall hexagonal steeple came down in 1941. Listed on the National Register in 2019.',
  'Per the Register form the interior is notably intact: curved pews in four sections on radiating aisles round a semicircular raised platform with the pulpit, beaded-board finishes, the pipes of an organ that no longer works, and windows of coloured glass in foliate and geometric patterns, one of them a true stained-glass dedication window. Interior seen at services only as far as checked; times not checked.',
  '',
  'At 129 Second Street in the village of Deposit, which straddles the Broome and Delaware county line. Look from the street; service times not checked. Street parking and the village lots on Monument Street.',
  [NP('100004734')],brick='?'),
N('hotel-delaware-east-branch','Hotel Delaware, East Branch','East Branch','cat','hotel','standing',41.98806,-75.13444,'site','view','drive','hotels','1891',
  'A three-storey wood-framed hotel built in 1891 and enlarged after a fire in 1908, 40 by 70 feet, from the hamlet\'s days as a stop by the East Branch of the Delaware. Listed on the National Register in 2004.',
  'The two-storey verandah across the front, on Doric columns at ground level. What it is used for now, and the interior, not checked.',
  '',
  'Current owner and use not checked; treat it as private and look from the road. Parking not checked.',
  [W('Hotel Delaware'),L('Delaware County NY Genealogy and History: Hotel Delaware postcards','https://dcnyhistory.org/hotel-delaware-postcards.html')],brick='no'),
N('lordville-presbyterian','Lordville Presbyterian Church','Lordville','cat','worship','standing',41.86900,-75.21468,'area','view','drive','','1896',
  'A small one-storey frame church built in 1896 in the river hamlet of Lordville, with a steep gable roof and an open, square belfry. Listed on the National Register in 2000.',
  'The frame church and its open belfry on Lordville Road. Interior not checked; whether it is still used not checked.',
  '',
  'On Lordville Road in the hamlet; look from the road. Owner and use not checked. Parking not checked: do not block the road.',
  [W('Lordville Presbyterian Church')],brick='no'),
N('rock-valley-school','Rock Valley School','Rock Valley','cat','civic','standing',41.89236,-75.08444,'site','view','drive','','1885',
  'A one-room school built in 1885, one storey of wood on a cut-stone foundation, about 24 by 36 feet. It taught into the early 1940s and since the 1950s has been the hamlet\'s polling place and community meeting house. Listed on the National Register in 2008.',
  'The gable-roofed schoolhouse on its cut-stone base. Interior not checked; it opens for votes and meetings.',
  '',
  'A community building in the hamlet of Rock Valley; look from the road. Owner and opening times not checked.',
  [W('Rock Valley School')],brick='no'),
N('cannonsville-hamlets','Cannonsville Reservoir: the drowned hamlets','Cannonsville','cat','town','water',42.08053,-75.32166,'exact','view','drive','drowned,cemeteries','flooded 1955–1967',
  'New York City built the Cannonsville Reservoir between 1955 and 1967 by damming the West Branch of the Delaware. It took Cannonsville, the valley\'s largest community with Presbyterian, Baptist and Methodist churches and a community house, and the hamlets of Granton, Rock Royal, Rock Rift and Beerston, with dozens of farms. More than 900 people were moved and 2,000 graves relocated. The Rock Rift acid factory made wood alcohol and housed many of the hamlet\'s 200 people; a stone barn at Rock Royal had to be dynamited. Two documentaries, Indian Summer (1960) and The Fall of Cannonsville (2023), record the end of the village.',
  'Two interpretive panels, "Reflections on Five Hamlets" and "The Cannonsville Reservoir", with old photographs, stand at this spot; the pin is their HMdb position. The water itself is all there is to see of the hamlets; the Rock Rift fire tower above the reservoir is a separate Atlas place.',
  'Five hamlets under one reservoir.',
  'New York City DEP land. The panels are at a paved roadside pull-off by the shore (seen on the aerial); look from there. Any other shoreline or reservoir land needs a free DEP Access Permit, and boats a DEP boat tag (Catskill Watershed Corporation boating sheet; DEP recreation, 1-800-575-5263).',
  [W('Cannonsville, New York'),W('Cannonsville Reservoir'),HM('241779','Reflections on Five Hamlets'),HM('137644','The Cannonsville Reservoir'),L('Catskill Watershed Corporation: Cannonsville boating','https://cwconline.org/wp-content/uploads/2022/05/FINAL-CANNONSVILLE.pdf')],brick='no'),
N('hankins-stone-arch-bridge','Hankins Stone Arch Bridge','Hankins','cat','landmark','standing',41.81664,-75.09153,'exact','view','drive','','1892',
  'A single stone arch over Hankins Creek, built of local stone by Wallace LaValley in 1892 for travellers before State Route 97 was laid out. A sister bridge at Basket was lost to a flood. This one was restored to its first form in 1999, and the Basket Historical Society put up its markers in 2002.',
  'The arch and its markers by Route 94 at Makela Road, in the hamlet of Hankins (Town of Fremont, Sullivan County).',
  '',
  'View from the roadside at Route 94 and Makela Road. Who owns the bridge and whether it carries traffic not checked. No mapped parking: pull well off the road only where it is safe.',
  [HM('222481','Hankins Stone Arch Bridge'),HM('222478','Hankins Creek Stone Arch Bridge')],brick='no'),
]

